1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,043
Every company in the AI race
is trying to beat the others.

2
00:00:04,083 --> 00:00:07,891
But there is one company that
gets paid no matter who wins.

3
00:00:08,681 --> 00:00:10,660
Nvidia. Google. Amazon. AMD.

4
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:16,139
They compete fiercely to build the chips
that power artificial intelligence.

5
00:00:16,179 --> 00:00:21,474
Yet the most advanced of those chips
are manufactured by the same company.

6
00:00:22,263 --> 00:00:24,467
Its name isn't printed on the chips,

7
00:00:24,507 --> 00:00:26,898
and most people have never heard of it.

8
00:00:26,938 --> 00:00:30,825
This is the story of the company
at the bottom of the AI stack,

9
00:00:30,865 --> 00:00:33,443
and why the entire industry depends on it.

10
00:00:34,234 --> 00:00:35,776
It's called TSMC,

11
00:00:35,816 --> 00:00:40,149
the Taiwan Semiconductor
Manufacturing Company.

12
00:00:40,189 --> 00:00:47,872
In
2025, it took in about 122 billion dollars in revenue, up 36 percent in a single

13
00:00:47,912 --> 00:00:48,412
year.

14
00:00:51,727 --> 00:00:53,017
To understand why,

15
00:00:53,057 --> 00:00:54,791
follow a single AI chip.

16
00:00:54,831 --> 00:00:57,156
Companies like Nvidia design it.

17
00:00:57,196 --> 00:00:59,595
But a design is only a blueprint.

18
00:00:59,635 --> 00:01:05,063
Someone has to turn it into billions
of transistors on a slice of silicon.

19
00:01:05,103 --> 00:01:07,354
For the most advanced AI chips,

20
00:01:07,394 --> 00:01:09,941
that someone is almost always TSMC.

21
00:01:10,731 --> 00:01:14,051
This business model is
the source of its power.

22
00:01:14,091 --> 00:01:15,338
TSMC is a foundry.

23
00:01:15,378 --> 00:01:18,269
It doesn't sell chips under its own name,

24
00:01:18,309 --> 00:01:21,271
and it doesn't compete with its customers.

25
00:01:21,311 --> 00:01:23,630
It makes chips for everyone else.

26
00:01:24,420 --> 00:01:27,259
And everyone else is almost everyone.

27
00:01:27,299 --> 00:01:30,526
According to the research firm TrendForce,

28
00:01:30,566 --> 00:01:36,439
TSMC earned close to 70 percent of all
foundry revenue in the world in 2025.

29
00:01:37,229 --> 00:01:39,446
Its nearest rival, Samsung,

30
00:01:39,486 --> 00:01:43,040
had about 7 percent.
China's SMIC, about 5.

31
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:44,545
That isn't a lead.

32
00:01:44,585 --> 00:01:46,551
It's a different league.

33
00:01:46,591 --> 00:01:52,151
Numbers two and three combined
don't reach a fifth of TSMC's share.

34
00:01:52,942 --> 00:01:55,589
Here is why that matters for the AI race.

35
00:01:55,629 --> 00:01:58,867
Nvidia might lose ground
to Google's custom chips.

36
00:01:58,907 --> 00:02:00,636
Amazon might build its own.

37
00:02:00,676 --> 00:02:03,258
A startup might design something better.

38
00:02:04,048 --> 00:02:06,830
In almost every one of those scenarios,

39
00:02:06,870 --> 00:02:09,000
the order still lands at TSMC.

40
00:02:09,789 --> 00:02:14,159
You can see the shift in
TSMC's own numbers. In 2025,

41
00:02:14,199 --> 00:02:16,405
high-performance computing,

42
00:02:16,445 --> 00:02:19,983
the category that
includes AI accelerators,

43
00:02:20,023 --> 00:02:22,812
made up 58 percent of its revenue.

44
00:02:23,603 --> 00:02:27,998
That's up from 51 percent a
year earlier. Smartphones,

45
00:02:28,038 --> 00:02:31,283
the business that built the modern TSMC,

46
00:02:31,323 --> 00:02:34,240
fell to 29 percent. Everything else,

47
00:02:34,280 --> 00:02:36,786
from cars to connected devices,

48
00:02:36,826 --> 00:02:39,086
adds up to about 13 percent.

49
00:02:42,476 --> 00:02:45,063
There's an old saying about gold rushes.

50
00:02:45,103 --> 00:02:48,347
The surest way to get rich
isn't digging for gold.

51
00:02:48,387 --> 00:02:49,989
It's selling the shovels.

52
00:02:50,779 --> 00:02:54,604
Nvidia is often called
the shovel seller of AI,

53
00:02:54,644 --> 00:02:57,975
and it has been spectacularly successful.

54
00:02:58,015 --> 00:03:00,031
For its fiscal year 2026,

55
00:03:00,071 --> 00:03:04,471
Nvidia reported nearly 216
billion dollars in revenue.

56
00:03:05,261 --> 00:03:07,493
But Nvidia designs its chips.

57
00:03:07,533 --> 00:03:10,548
It doesn't manufacture them. TSMC does.

58
00:03:10,588 --> 00:03:13,603
So it is with Google's custom AI chips,

59
00:03:13,643 --> 00:03:15,013
Amazon's Trainium,

60
00:03:15,053 --> 00:03:16,814
and AMD's accelerators.

61
00:03:16,854 --> 00:03:21,201
The shovel sellers all buy
from the same shovel factory.

62
00:03:21,990 --> 00:03:25,259
And the dependence goes
beyond the silicon itself.

63
00:03:25,299 --> 00:03:29,295
Modern AI chips need an advanced
packaging step called CoWoS,

64
00:03:29,335 --> 00:03:33,199
which places the processor
right next to high-speed memory.

65
00:03:33,989 --> 00:03:37,581
By one estimate from the
research group Epoch AI,

66
00:03:37,621 --> 00:03:43,806
just
four companies used more than 90 percent of the world's CoWoS capacity in 2025.

67
00:03:44,596 --> 00:03:49,765
That packaging capacity is one of the
tightest bottlenecks in the industry.

68
00:03:49,805 --> 00:03:52,890
Which means TSMC doesn't
just make the chips.

69
00:03:52,930 --> 00:03:57,404
In practice, it helps decide how
many of them the world can have.

70
00:04:00,795 --> 00:04:03,199
But even TSMC depends on someone.

71
00:04:03,239 --> 00:04:09,197
To print the tiniest features on a chip,
it uses extreme ultraviolet lithography.

72
00:04:09,237 --> 00:04:13,271
And only one company in the
world sells those machines:

73
00:04:13,311 --> 00:04:15,122
ASML, in the Netherlands.

74
00:04:15,911 --> 00:04:17,444
Its newest machines,

75
00:04:17,484 --> 00:04:18,938
called High-NA EUV,

76
00:04:18,978 --> 00:04:23,263
reportedly cost around 380
to 400 million dollars each.

77
00:04:23,303 --> 00:04:26,015
That is the price of a single tool.

78
00:04:26,806 --> 00:04:31,945
So the real structure of the AI
economy looks like this. At the top,

79
00:04:31,985 --> 00:04:35,677
the AI products everyone
talks about. Below them,

80
00:04:35,717 --> 00:04:38,495
the chip designers. Below them, TSMC.

81
00:04:38,535 --> 00:04:40,170
And underneath it all,

82
00:04:40,210 --> 00:04:42,988
machines from a single Dutch company.

83
00:04:46,378 --> 00:04:50,443
Now for the part that keeps
governments awake at night.

84
00:04:50,483 --> 00:04:56,563
A 2021 study by the Semiconductor Industry
Association and Boston Consulting Group

85
00:04:56,603 --> 00:05:01,863
found that 92 percent of the world's
most advanced chipmaking capacity,

86
00:05:01,903 --> 00:05:04,475
below 10 nanometers, was in Taiwan.

87
00:05:05,265 --> 00:05:06,501
Definitions vary,

88
00:05:06,541 --> 00:05:08,528
and that share is shifting.

89
00:05:08,568 --> 00:05:10,105
But the point stands.

90
00:05:10,895 --> 00:05:15,720
A huge part of the AI economy rests on
factories on one island, in an active

91
00:05:15,760 --> 00:05:20,842
earthquake zone, at the center of one of
the world's most sensitive geopolitical

92
00:05:20,882 --> 00:05:21,418
disputes.

93
00:05:22,208 --> 00:05:27,196
Which is why the map is starting
to change. In March 2025,

94
00:05:27,236 --> 00:05:34,391
TSMC said it expects its total investment
in the United States to reach 165 billion

95
00:05:34,431 --> 00:05:35,085
dollars.

96
00:05:35,875 --> 00:05:40,798
Its first Arizona factory entered
volume production in late 2024.

97
00:05:40,838 --> 00:05:43,317
The new plan adds more factories,

98
00:05:43,357 --> 00:05:47,287
two advanced packaging
plants and a research center.

99
00:05:48,077 --> 00:05:52,576
But notice who is building those
factories. It's still TSMC.

100
00:05:52,616 --> 00:05:56,736
Moving production closer to
customers reduces one risk.

101
00:05:56,776 --> 00:06:00,216
It doesn't change who
the industry depends on.

102
00:06:03,606 --> 00:06:04,753
So who benefits?

103
00:06:04,793 --> 00:06:10,685
TSMC and ASML collect something like a
toll on nearly every advanced AI chip, no

104
00:06:10,725 --> 00:06:13,355
matter whose design wins. Who loses?

105
00:06:13,395 --> 00:06:17,804
Rival manufacturers fighting
over what's left of the market,

106
00:06:17,844 --> 00:06:22,402
and every chip designer that has
to wait in line for capacity.

107
00:06:23,192 --> 00:06:26,940
And TSMC is betting big that this lasts.

108
00:06:26,980 --> 00:06:33,759
It plans to spend between 52 and 56
billion dollars on capacity in 2026.

109
00:06:34,549 --> 00:06:39,661
Its
chief executive has reportedly admitted to being very nervous about whether AI

110
00:06:39,701 --> 00:06:40,729
demand will hold.

111
00:06:41,519 --> 00:06:43,842
So here is what to watch. First,

112
00:06:43,882 --> 00:06:48,937
whether AI demand justifies record
spending on new factories. Second,

113
00:06:48,977 --> 00:06:52,112
whether packaging
capacity keeps up. Third,

114
00:06:52,152 --> 00:06:56,985
whether factories outside Taiwan
can match its output. And fourth,

115
00:06:57,025 --> 00:06:59,643
whether any rival can close the gap.

116
00:07:00,433 --> 00:07:05,280
The
AI race will produce winners and losers among the companies we talk about every

117
00:07:05,320 --> 00:07:05,820
day.

118
00:07:06,306 --> 00:07:11,394
But the most dependable money may not
go to whoever builds the smartest model.

119
00:07:11,434 --> 00:07:14,812
It may go to whoever owns
the bottleneck. Right now,

120
00:07:14,852 --> 00:07:18,165
that's a chipmaker most
people have never heard of.

121
00:07:18,955 --> 00:07:20,499
This is THE NEXT SHIFT.

122
00:07:20,539 --> 00:07:23,115
Understand tomorrow before it happens.
